WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.540
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.980
So now the theory about Higgs
boson is quite interesting.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:20.980
And we have seen that it's
able to produce masses of boson

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.290
and of fermions.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.990
But as Feynman said, a theory is
only as good as the experiment.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.330
And so we have to
actually find Higgs boson

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.880
and measure their properties
and see whether or not

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.150
their properties are consistent
with the standard model.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.160
And so the first
question we have to ask

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.440
is how Higgs boson
is actually produced

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.410
and how they might decay.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.380
So this little video is
just talking about this

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.280
in very simple terms.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.450
So we are able to
produce Higgs boson

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.420
at proton-proton machines,
at proton-proton colliders,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.110
because they are able to
bring collisions or produce

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:03.550
collisions which allow to
have energies of a mass scale

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.349
consistent with the Higgs boson.

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:07.150
We already know now
that the Higgs boson

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.400
has a mass of 125 GeV.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.070
So the collisions have
to have enough energy

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.170
to produce this particle.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.590
We're using protons to
do this because protons

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.640
can be accelerated easier
than electrons, for example,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.290
to those energies.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.650
But protons themselves
are, as we have seen,

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.820
objects which consist
of quarks and gluons.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.340
So how do gluons now
produce Higgs bosons?

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.140
And this is shown in
this Feynman diagram

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.150
here where you have two gluons
from two colliding protons.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:46.610
And they are able via this
loop diagram, triangle diagram,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.450
including the top quark-- the
top quark goes around here--

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.120
to cause an excitation
in the vacuum,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.850
which is the Higgs boson.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:01.200
They couple to the vacuum
producing the Higgs boson.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.630
And then instantaneously,
the Higgs boson decays.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.310
And it can do this
in various ways.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.300
And we look at this
on the next slide.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.160
And in this example, it decays
via a similar triangle loop

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.500
into a W. And then the W
is electrically charged

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.150
and can radiate a
pair of photons.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.600
So while the gluons and
the photons are massless,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.680
we are able to
produce Higgs bosons

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.530
via the collisions of
two gluons and observe it

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.610
via decay into photons.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.420
This is quite spectacular.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.760
In more general ways, it's
not just one mechanism

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.040
to produce Higgs bosons,
but there's various.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.450
And the leading
ones are shown here.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.360
We have just looked at
this first one, which

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.360
is called gluon fusion
channel, so two gluons

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.540
fuse together to a Higgs boson.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.130
There's this one here,
the second one, which

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.360
is called Vector
Boson Fusion, BBF,

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.950
where we have the quarks
radiate to vector bosons.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.123
This is either the Z
boson or the W boson.

00:03:04.123 --> 00:03:05.540
And then those
couple to the Higgs

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.940
field and produce
the Higgs boson.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.490
We can also have
associated production

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:15.620
where we have two quarks
via, again, a Z boson or a W

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.430
boson radiating then
a Z boson or a W boson

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.980
and generating a Higgs.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.450
And then a very exciting
one is the last one here

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:30.390
where the Higgs boson is
produced in association

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.500
with two top quarks.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.130
Remember, the top quark
here has a mass of 175 GeV.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.850
The mass of the Higgs
boson is 125 GeV.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.090
And so we have two of those.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.820
So the scale of this event
is in the order of 500 GeV.

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:52.130
This plot here shows the
production cross-section

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.040
as functional center
of mass energy.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.570
Now the Large Hadron
Collider, the LHC,

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.720
operates currently at 13 TeV.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.860
So the cross-section we
want to look at those.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.340
So you see that the leading
cross-section, which

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.740
is in the order of
tens of picobarn,

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.946
is the one where we
have gluon fusion.

00:04:09.946 --> 00:04:12.605
On the order of
magnitude less is the one

00:04:12.605 --> 00:04:14.070
with vector boson fusion.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.230
And then we have
associated production.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.480
And then the last
one is the one where

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.660
we have Higgs boson produced
in association with top quarks.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.230
Because the mass
scale is much higher,

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.280
the cross-section for
TD Higgs is lower.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.560
If you were to increase
the center of mass energy

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.320
of the LHC, you see this rapid
increase in cross-sections

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:36.910
just because there's
more phase base

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.430
available for this production.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:45.390
The coupling here and the
coupling here is the same.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.430
Then the Higgs boson,
as I said, decays.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.070
And we have already discussed
Higgs branching ratios,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.200
or branching ratios in general.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.235
And since the coupling of
the Higgs boson to fermions

00:04:56.235 --> 00:04:58.380
is proportional to the
mass of the fermions,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.030
you see the dominant decay is
the one into Higgs to bb-bar.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.280
And then you can
find lower decays

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.060
to the taus, the [INAUDIBLE],,
and to muons here.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.490
You also have to into the
vector bosons, W-W, and Z-Z.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.980
So similar triangle
diagrams, you

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.390
have decays into glu-glu,
and then to photons as well.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:25.110
So we have already measured
the Higgs boson at 125 GeV.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.970
And so those are the
branching ratios as

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.720
predicted in the standard model.

